import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.yZB1U4Nl.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Design decisions","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"design-decisions.md","filePath":"design-decisions.md"}'),n={name:"design-decisions.md"};function r(i,e,d,c,h,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="design-decisions" tabindex="-1">Design decisions <a class="header-anchor" href="#design-decisions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design decisions&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The choices that shape the engine, each as decision, reason and what it costs. Nothing here is a roadmap; it is why the code looks the way it does.</p><h2 id="a-reducer-kernel-instead-of-replayed-linear-code" tabindex="-1">A reducer kernel instead of replayed linear code <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-reducer-kernel-instead-of-replayed-linear-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A reducer kernel instead of replayed linear code&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> A workflow is <code>(state, event) → (state, commands)</code>. State is the snapshot; the kernel folds one event per transition and never runs workflow code otherwise.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Linear <code>await</code> code has to be re-executed from the top to resume, which needs a frozen realm or sandbox for determinism, a poll or asyncify contract to suspend, and replay cost proportional to history. A reducer never suspends: there is no stack to save, no realm to freeze, and recovery is &quot;load the checkpoint, verify its hash, continue&quot;.</p><p><strong>Consequence.</strong> Pipelines are written as phases (<code>sequence</code>, <code>fanOut</code>, <code>waitFor</code>, <code>sleep</code>) or as hand-written handlers, not as <code>await a(); await b();</code>. A linear layer could be built later on top of the same journal; it would not change the kernel.</p><h2 id="the-journal-is-the-queue-brokers-only-as-a-transport" tabindex="-1">The journal is the queue; brokers only as a transport <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-journal-is-the-queue-brokers-only-as-a-transport" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The journal is the queue; brokers only as a transport&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> The <code>tasks</code> table is the outbox and the lease. There is no message broker in the write path. <code>TaskTransport</code> (HTTP push today, in-process for a local worker) is delivery only; the row is the only source of truth.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A broker gives you a second source of truth, an ack deadline that caps task duration, and one more service on the hot path. A row committed in the same transaction as the event gives exactly-once recording, lease-and-heartbeat with no duration ceiling, completion from anywhere at any time, and a crash between &quot;persisted&quot; and &quot;pushed&quot; that loses nothing.</p><p><strong>Consequence.</strong> Fan-out throughput is bounded by the database (low thousands of tasks per second on Postgres). Teams with an existing broker can add a transport adapter that carries the pushed task; the journal still records it.</p><h2 id="rust-kernel-on-wasm-with-a-size-budget-and-a-semantic-gate" tabindex="-1">Rust kernel on wasm, with a size budget and a semantic gate <a class="header-anchor" href="#rust-kernel-on-wasm-with-a-size-budget-and-a-semantic-gate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rust kernel on wasm, with a size budget and a semantic gate&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> The kernel is Rust with zero I/O, compiled to <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> via <code>wasm-bindgen</code>, committed under <code>sdk/ts/sdk/wasm/</code>, capped at 1 MiB. CI rebuilds it and requires both the committed and the fresh artifact to pass every golden fixture.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> One implementation of the envelope rules, hashing and retry maths serves Node now and any other host later, and a pure library is the easiest thing to test exhaustively (proptests, an exhaustive step-edge table, fixtures). Byte-identical wasm across macOS and Linux toolchains is not achievable in practice, so the gate is on behaviour instead.</p><p><strong>Consequence.</strong> JS consumers never need a Rust toolchain. Kernel changes require rebuilding and committing the wasm and regenerating fixtures; <code>VLO_KERNEL_WASM</code> lets you test another build in place.</p><h2 id="a-write-set-kernel-the-kernel-returns-rows-the-host-writes-them" tabindex="-1">A write-set kernel: the kernel returns rows, the host writes them <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-write-set-kernel-the-kernel-returns-rows-the-host-writes-them" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A write-set kernel: the kernel returns rows, the host writes them&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> <code>reduce_step</code> returns a declarative <code>Transition</code> — events, checkpoint, new tasks, task updates, timers, spawns, signal waits, emits, completion — and the journal applies it atomically without interpreting commands.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A kernel that owns the database cannot compile to wasm and would force a native host. Splitting &quot;decide&quot; from &quot;persist&quot; keeps the kernel pure and testable, and makes every journal adapter a dumb applier of the same shape.</p><p><strong>Consequence.</strong> Journal adapters are small (one SQL text for both dialects) and can be conformance-tested with <code>journalConformance</code>. Cross-run effects (starting a child, notifying a parent) happen in later transactions through the outbox, never inside the fold.</p><h2 id="hashed-json-is-kept-as-text" tabindex="-1">Hashed JSON is kept as text <a class="header-anchor" href="#hashed-json-is-kept-as-text" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Hashed JSON is kept as text&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> <code>state</code>, <code>kernel_state</code>, <code>payload</code>, <code>input</code> and <code>output</code> are <code>text</code> columns. Only <code>search</code>, <code>usage</code> and <code>retry_policy</code> are <code>jsonb</code> on Postgres.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>jsonb</code> normalises key order and numeric lexemes. The re-fold check compares the hash of what the kernel wrote with the hash of what it reads back; any rewrite in between is a false nondeterminism.</p><p><strong>Consequence.</strong> You cannot index into state with <code>jsonb</code> operators. Put queryable attributes in <code>search</code> when starting a run.</p><h2 id="file-and-gcs-blobs-with-a-256-kib-offload" tabindex="-1">File and GCS blobs, with a 256 KiB offload <a class="header-anchor" href="#file-and-gcs-blobs-with-a-256-kib-offload" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;File and GCS blobs, with a 256 KiB offload&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> Anything larger than 256 KiB leaves the journal for a content-addressed blob store; <code>file://</code> and <code>gcs://</code> are built in. References are <code>blob://sha256/&lt;hex&gt;</code>.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> History rows should stay small enough that folding thousands of events is cheap, and a content address makes retries and duplicates free. GCS with Application Default Credentials matches the first production target; a directory matches a laptop and CI.</p><p><strong>Consequence.</strong> Every oversized payload costs a blob round trip on the write path. Other stores (S3, Vercel Blob) are adapters to add; the interface is two methods.</p><h2 id="push-by-default-signed-with-an-hmac" tabindex="-1">Push by default, signed with an HMAC <a class="header-anchor" href="#push-by-default-signed-with-an-hmac" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Push by default, signed with an HMAC&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> The dispatcher POSTs the full task to the queue&#39;s URL and expects <code>202</code>; the worker completes later through <code>complete_url</code>. Every push carries <code>vlo-signature: v1=hmac-sha256(secret, task_id.attempt.ts.sha256(body))</code> and is refused outside a five-minute window. Pull (<code>mode: &quot;pull&quot;</code>) and in-process are the alternatives.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Push lets workers scale to zero and live in serverless functions, and a signature bound to task, attempt and time means a queue URL alone cannot be fed forged or replayed tasks. Pull exists for workers behind a NAT or on hardware the orchestrator cannot reach.</p><p><strong>Consequence.</strong> Every push queue needs a shared <code>secret</code>; the dispatcher refuses to push unsigned. Workers must be idempotent on <code>idempotency_key</code>: delivery is at-least-once.</p><h2 id="a-manual-dispatcher-plus-a-cron-for-serverless" tabindex="-1">A manual dispatcher plus a cron for serverless <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-manual-dispatcher-plus-a-cron-for-serverless" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A manual dispatcher plus a cron for serverless&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> <code>dispatcher: { mode: &quot;manual&quot; }</code> never arms an interval and ignores <code>kick()</code>. Progress comes from <code>host.drain({ maxMs })</code>, called by the request that started or signalled a run, and from a cron hitting <code>POST /v1/timers/tick?drain=1</code>.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A serverless platform may freeze the process the moment a response is sent. An unattended background tick would leave tasks marked <code>running</code> with nobody behind them; an awaited, time-boxed drain finishes what it started, and the cron catches whatever a crashed invocation left in the outbox.</p><p><strong>Consequence.</strong> A run&#39;s chain of in-process steps advances within one invocation; anything that parks (a timer, a remote worker, a signal) waits at most one cron interval. <code>vlo doctor</code> warns when a manual orchestrator has no <code>deploy.cron</code>.</p><h2 id="budget-is-a-reducer-decision" tabindex="-1">Budget is a reducer decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#budget-is-a-reducer-decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Budget is a reducer decision&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> The kernel banks every attempt&#39;s <code>usage</code> into <code>usage_total</code> and hands it to the reducer on every event. Budget enforcement lives in <code>@oona/vlo/agent</code>, which parks an exhausted run on a top-up signal instead of failing it.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> An engine-level interceptor would need policy the kernel cannot know (what a token costs, what a ceiling means, whether to park or fail). Trustworthy accounting is the only part that must be engine-level, because a reducer cannot see a failed attempt&#39;s spend any other way.</p><p><strong>Consequence.</strong> Any workflow, not just an agent, can read <code>usage_total</code> and decide. There is no price table: <code>cost_micro_usd</code> is whatever the worker reports.</p><h2 id="one-checkpoint-verified-by-hash-rebuilt-from-zero" tabindex="-1">One checkpoint, verified by hash, rebuilt from zero <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-checkpoint-verified-by-hash-rebuilt-from-zero" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One checkpoint, verified by hash, rebuilt from zero&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> <code>run_state</code> holds exactly one checkpoint per run, rewritten in the same transaction as every event. On load the host re-hashes it; a mismatch, a missing row or a different bundle digest triggers a full fold from <code>seq 0</code>, comparing each transition&#39;s hash with the journaled one.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Folding is calling a pure function once per input event, which is milliseconds for thousands of events, so a &quot;last N events&quot; window buys nothing. A per-event hash pinpoints the exact seq where a reducer stopped agreeing with its own history.</p><p><strong>Consequence.</strong> A run failed with <code>nondeterminism_detected</code> is a bug in the reducer, never retried. Long-lived runs compact history with <code>continue_as_new</code>.</p>',42)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
